12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1a",null,{"guide":{"date":"March 7–8, 1862, with campaign consequences through July 1862","place":"Elkhorn Tavern and the Telegraph Road at Pea Ridge, Arkansas; retreat routes to Van Buren; later movements to Corinth and Helena","result":"United States victory. Van Dorn captured Elkhorn Tavern late on March 7 but could not break Curtis’s army. On March 8, Curtis attacked with all four divisions while the Confederate ammunition train remained hours away. Van Dorn retreated, and Confederate forces did not regain control of northern Arkansas or Missouri","facts":[{"label":"United States strength on March 8","value":"About 10,000 men in a line nearly one mile long"},{"label":"United States casualties","value":"1,384; more than half in Carr’s division"},{"label":"Estimated Confederate casualties","value":"About 2,000 in the battle, plus additional losses during the marches"},{"label":"Confederate supply train","value":"Five or six hours from the battlefield when the March 8 attack began"},{"label":"Van Buren from Pea Ridge","value":"About 90 miles directly, but farther by the available retreat routes"},{"label":"Territory secured","value":"Northern Arkansas and Missouri remained under United States control"}],"maps":[{"title":"Elkhorn Tavern today","caption":"The Telegraph Road runs north and south past the tavern. Huntsville Road enters from the east, and Ford Road approaches south of the tavern from the west. The preserved battlefield includes the ground over which both armies fought on March 7–8.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-94.055%2C36.435%2C-93.925%2C36.505&layer=mapnik&marker=36.469%2C-94.021"},{"title":"Positions on March 8","caption":"The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers map shows the final positions around Elkhorn Tavern. Curtis formed all four divisions south and west of the Confederate line, then used concentrated artillery and an infantry advance to force Van Dorn from the field.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447263/","image":"/images/pea-ridge-battlefield-map.jpg","alt":"1876 map of the Pea Ridge battlefield showing United States and Confederate positions on March 8, 1862"},{"title":"How the first day produced the final line","caption":"On March 7, Van Dorn and Price pushed south toward Elkhorn Tavern while McCulloch’s separate attack failed at Leetown. Curtis moved the Army of the Southwest from Little Sugar Creek and turned it to face northeast for the second day.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-3-leetown-battlefield.htm","image":"/images/pea-ridge-march-7-map.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the March 7 fighting at Elkhorn Tavern and Leetown with United States forces in blue and Confederate forces in red"},{"title":"Campaign and retreat routes","caption":"The National Park Service map shows Pea Ridge between the Missouri line and the Boston Mountains. Confederate groups retreated by the Telegraph Road, Bentonville Detour, and Huntsville Road before moving toward Van Buren.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm","image":"/images/pea-ridge-campaign-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Pea Ridge campaign in Missouri and Arkansas"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:07","audioTime":67,"title":"The Leetown fight had removed half of Van Dorn’s army","detail":"McCulloch’s division was defeated and fragmented west of Elkhorn Tavern. Van Dorn and Price continued fighting for the Telegraph Road with about 5,000 men."},{"time":"2:02","audioTime":122,"title":"Three roads shaped the Elkhorn Tavern action","detail":"The Telegraph Road ran north and south. Huntsville Road entered near the tavern from the east, while Ford Road joined from the west about 500 yards south."},{"time":"3:35","audioTime":215,"title":"Van Dorn and Price attacked through Cross Timber Hollow","detail":"Their force moved south on the Telegraph Road and encountered Carr’s division in rugged ground immediately north of Elkhorn Tavern."},{"time":"4:33","audioTime":273,"title":"Van Dorn learned that McCulloch was heavily engaged","detail":"At about 2 p.m., an officer reported fighting on Ford Road. Van Dorn could no longer assume that McCulloch would soon reinforce the attack at the tavern."},{"time":"5:02","audioTime":302,"title":"Sterling Price was wounded","detail":"A bullet struck Price in the right arm and side. His aides bandaged the wound with handkerchiefs, and he remained on the field."},{"time":"5:15","audioTime":315,"title":"Curtis committed most of the army northward","detail":"With no Confederates appearing at Little Sugar Creek, Curtis accepted that the main threat was in his rear and ordered additional forces toward Elkhorn Tavern."},{"time":"5:45","audioTime":345,"title":"The final Confederate assault began at about 4:30 p.m.","detail":"Van Dorn directed the right and Price the left. Their troops attacked Carr’s thin line in and above Cross Timber Hollow before United States reinforcements could fully arrive."},{"time":"6:17","audioTime":377,"title":"Carr withdrew south of Elkhorn Tavern","detail":"Confederates emerged from the hollow and threatened to surround the defenders. Carr moved to a fence line south of the tavern."},{"time":"6:32","audioTime":392,"title":"The Confederates captured the tavern but stopped","detail":"Broken formations, difficult ground, hunger, and fatigue ended the advance. Soldiers searched the tavern and outbuildings for food with only about 30 minutes of daylight remaining."},{"time":"7:03","audioTime":423,"title":"Curtis counterattacked at dusk","detail":"Curtis reached the field and directed an attack that recovered some ground but did not retake Elkhorn Tavern. The United States line remained intact."},{"time":"7:41","audioTime":461,"title":"The first day ended without a decision","detail":"The Confederates held Elkhorn Tavern, but Curtis had prevented his army from being destroyed. The fighting at Leetown had already eliminated much of Van Dorn’s striking force."},{"time":"8:02","audioTime":482,"title":"Curtis reversed the Army of the Southwest","detail":"During March 7, the army fought two separated actions, protected its wagon train, moved back about two and a half miles, and turned almost 200 degrees to face northeast."},{"time":"9:33","audioTime":573,"title":"Curtis formed all four divisions for March 8","detail":"During the night, the divisions assembled south of the Confederate position and centered their line on the Telegraph Road."},{"time":"9:52","audioTime":592,"title":"The Confederate ammunition remained on the detour","detail":"Van Dorn’s supply and ordnance train had not reached the battlefield. Artillery batteries and infantry cartridge boxes had little ammunition available for another full day."},{"time":"10:19","audioTime":619,"title":"The numerical advantage reversed","detail":"Van Dorn and Price had outnumbered Carr about two to one for much of March 7. On March 8, all four United States divisions outnumbered the remaining Confederate force about two to one."},{"time":"10:41","audioTime":641,"title":"Curtis launched the March 8 attack","detail":"About 10,000 United States troops advanced from a line nearly one mile long. Van Dorn learned that his ammunition wagons were still five or six hours away."},{"time":"10:56","audioTime":656,"title":"Van Dorn ordered a retreat","detail":"With artillery ammunition almost gone and no means to stop Curtis’s advance, Van Dorn told Price that the army had to leave the field."},{"time":"11:13","audioTime":673,"title":"The Confederate army withdrew in three groups","detail":"One group went north on the Telegraph Road, another withdrew with Martin Green’s wagon train by the Bentonville Detour, and Van Dorn and Price led the main body east on Huntsville Road."},{"time":"12:55","audioTime":775,"title":"Van Dorn arrested James Rains","detail":"Rains said that Van Dorn, rather than the soldiers, had been defeated. Van Dorn heard the remark and placed him under arrest."},{"time":"13:16","audioTime":796,"title":"The main Confederate column lacked food and shelter","detail":"Many men had gone three days without rations. The troops separated from the wagon train had few tents, blankets, or overcoats and had to cross the Boston Mountains in winter weather."},{"time":"13:58","audioTime":838,"title":"Van Dorn left the army during the retreat","detail":"Van Dorn departed with a small party and reached Van Buren two days later. Wounded Sterling Price remained responsible for moving the main column to safety."},{"time":"15:45","audioTime":945,"title":"The retreat continued for more than a week","detail":"Scattered Confederate groups crossed the Boston Mountains into the Arkansas River valley. Curtis remained near Pea Ridge for ten days before returning north to Keetsville, Missouri."},{"time":"16:11","audioTime":971,"title":"The episode gives the battle’s casualty totals","detail":"The Army of the Southwest reported 1,384 casualties, more than half in Carr’s division. Confederate losses were not officially reported and are estimated in the episode at about 2,000."},{"time":"16:45","audioTime":1005,"title":"The Army of the West was ordered east","detail":"On March 25, Albert Sidney Johnston ordered Van Dorn to Corinth. The army crossed the Mississippi too late to participate at Shiloh."},{"time":"17:28","audioTime":1048,"title":"Prairie Grove ended the next large Confederate attempt","detail":"Thomas Hindman built another army in northwest Arkansas later in 1862, but it was defeated at Prairie Grove in December. No organized Confederate force threatened Missouri again for two years."},{"time":"17:47","audioTime":1067,"title":"Curtis was nominated for promotion","detail":"Curtis was nominated for major general on March 27. His army later moved through eastern Arkansas and reached Helena on the Mississippi River on July 12."},{"time":"19:48","audioTime":1188,"title":"Pea Ridge secured the region","detail":"The victory maintained United States control of Missouri and northern Arkansas. Van Dorn’s army never again fought as a major force west of the Mississippi."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Samuel R. Curtis","command":"Army of the Southwest","subordinates":"Reversed the army and ordered the March 8 attack"},{"commander":"Franz Sigel","command":"1st and 2nd Divisions","subordinates":"Peter J. Osterhaus and Alexander Asboth"},{"commander":"Jefferson C. Davis","command":"3rd Division","subordinates":"Joined the final line south of Elkhorn Tavern"},{"commander":"Eugene A. Carr","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Grenville Dodge and William Vandever; bore most of the March 7 Elkhorn fighting"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Earl Van Dorn","command":"Army of the West","subordinates":"Directed the right at Elkhorn Tavern and ordered the retreat"},{"commander":"Sterling Price","command":"Missouri State Guard","subordinates":"Directed the Confederate left despite being wounded"},{"commander":"Martin E. Green","command":"Wagon-train guard","subordinates":"Led about 3,000 men and the train away by the detour"},{"commander":"Albert Pike","command":"Indian Territory brigade","subordinates":"With part of the retreat north of Elkhorn Tavern"},{"commander":"Benjamin McCulloch","command":"McCulloch’s Division","subordinates":"Killed at Leetown on March 7; most of the division did not rejoin Van Dorn"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Samuel R. Curtis","role":"Reoriented the Army of the Southwest, concentrated its artillery, and ordered the decisive March 8 attack","side":"United States","image":"/images/samuel-r-curtis.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018669095/"},{"name":"Earl Van Dorn","role":"Captured Elkhorn Tavern on March 7 but lost control of his logistics and ordered the retreat on March 8","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/earl-van-dorn.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/general-van-dorn.htm"},{"name":"Eugene A. Carr","role":"Held the Elkhorn Tavern sector against a larger force and suffered three wounds","side":"United States"},{"name":"Sterling Price","role":"Directed the Confederate left at Elkhorn Tavern after being wounded in the arm and side","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Grenville M. Dodge","role":"Led a brigade in Carr’s division during the defense of Elkhorn Tavern","side":"United States"},{"name":"Martin E. Green","role":"Brought the delayed Confederate supply train away with about 3,000 soldiers","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James S. Rains","role":"Missouri State Guard brigadier arrested after criticizing Van Dorn during the retreat","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas C. Hindman","role":"Later raised another Confederate army in Arkansas and was defeated at Prairie Grove","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Benjamin McCulloch","side":"Confederacy","role":"Division commander at Leetown","outcome":"Killed by United States skirmishers on March 7. His death contributed to the collapse of half of Van Dorn’s army."},{"name":"James McIntosh","side":"Confederacy","role":"Cavalry commander and McCulloch’s successor","outcome":"Killed at Leetown minutes after McCulloch."},{"name":"Sterling Price","side":"Confederacy","role":"Missouri State Guard commander","outcome":"Wounded in the right arm and side near Elkhorn Tavern on March 7; remained in command."},{"name":"Eugene A. Carr","side":"United States","role":"4th Division commander","outcome":"Wounded three times while directing the defense at Elkhorn Tavern; remained on the field."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-elkhorn-tavern.jpg","alt":"Reconstructed Elkhorn Tavern at Pea Ridge National Military Park","caption":"The reconstructed Elkhorn Tavern stands on the original site. The wartime building served as a hospital and briefly as Van Dorn’s headquarters.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-7-elkhorn-tavern.htm"},{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-east-overlook.jpg","alt":"View from the East Overlook across part of the Pea Ridge battlefield","caption":"The East Overlook looks across ground occupied by the Confederate line and attacked by Curtis on March 8.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-7-east-overlook-day-2.htm"},{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-battle-print.jpg","alt":"1889 chromolithograph of the Battle of Pea Ridge","caption":"Kurz & Allison’s 1889 print is a later artistic interpretation, not a contemporary battlefield view.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645343/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 104","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/104-pea-ridge-part-the-fourth"},{"label":"National Park Service: Elkhorn Tavern","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-7-elkhorn-tavern.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: East Overlook, day two","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-7-east-overlook-day-2.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Federal artillery, day two","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-10-federal-artillery-day-2.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: The outcome","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-outcome.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Pea Ridge order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Pea Ridge battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447263/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Battle of Pea Ridge print","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645343/"}]}}]}]
